  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nHigh pressure will continue to build into the area, becoming\ncentered over the lower Great Lakes region on Tuesday. Dry and\nseasonable weather is expected to continue.\n\nSouthwest flow develops on the backside of the departing high on\nWednesday, with above normal temperatures expected on Wednesday\nand Thursday as highs rise to the upper 70s Wednesday afternoon\nand around 80 Thursday afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA weakening southern-stream disturbance over the Southwest will\nbecome increasingly sheared through midweek, with associated\nmoisture spreading northeastward within strengthening\nsouthwesterly flow. A northern-stream trough crossing Ontario\nThursday into Friday will drive a cold front across the region.\nThe combination of increasing moisture and frontal forcing\nshould result in increasing shower changes Thursday night into \nFriday.\n\nModel guidance continues to trend towards a slower system with a\nlower QPF solution. Thursday is now expected to be dry with rain\nshowers with most of the mainstream global NWP starting to bring\nrain showers into the northern and western parts of the forecast\narea Thursday night. The AI models, including the ECWMF AIFS and\nAIGFS have slower solutions and have tended to outperform their\nphysically derived counterparts (GFS/ECMWF), so would expect\nthe forecast to continue trending drier Thursday night with a\nbetter chance of rain showers on Friday instead. Overall QPF is\nlikely to be light (most model guidance at or less than 0.5\"\nQPF) and thunderstorms are very unlikely due to the lack of\ninstability expected.",
